-- Update expanded explanations for 6.1-part5 (25 cases).
-- Safe to re-run: only updates tactical_explanations.

UPDATE public.economics_cases SET tactical_explanations = ARRAY['FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. An operating licence is classified as a current asset because, being intangible, it is easily converted into cash within a year.

The task tests the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Financial statements distinguish stocks (balance sheet at a date) from flows (income and cash over a period); many false statements swap those roles.

Apply the case evidence: An operating licence is a non-current intangible asset because it provides benefit over several years, not because it converts to cash quickly. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'FALSE — The claim is false. Because an operating licence cannot be touched, it is recorded as an expense rather than as an asset on the balance sheet.

The task tests the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Financial statements distinguish stocks (balance sheet at a date) from flows (income and cash over a period); many false statements swap those roles.

Apply the case evidence: An operating licence with expected long-term benefit is recorded as an intangible non-current asset, not expensed immediately. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

Watch the absolute wording "cannot": exam statements often sound plausible until a single scope word turns an otherwise familiar idea into an overclaim.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.', 'TRUE — The claim is correct. A concession right lacks physical substance but is classified as a non-current intangible asset because it provides expected long-term benefit.

The task tests the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Financial statements distinguish stocks (balance sheet at a date) from flows (income and cash over a period); many false statements swap those roles.

Apply the case evidence: Long-term expected benefit, not physical form, justifies classifying a concession right as non-current. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'FALSE — This statement is false. Non-current assets can never include an operating licence because that section is reserved strictly for tangible items such as machinery.

This is an accounting reasoning item on the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Decide whether the sentence correctly describes how a transaction, balance-sheet line, or period result affects assets, liabilities, or equity.

From the figures or classification rule involved, the non-current assets section includes intangible items such as an operating licence alongside tangible assets. Accounting statements fail when a loss is treated as increasing equity, when a flow is placed on the balance sheet, or when a ratio inequality is reversed.

Watch the absolute wording "never": exam statements often sound plausible until a single scope word turns an otherwise familiar idea into an overclaim.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. An operating licence is treated as inventory because it is intended for use rather than for display.

The underlying topic is the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. When figures appear, the exam checks whether you can interpret them in accounting terms—what increased, what ratio is implied, and whether the label (current, non-current, equity, and so on) fits.

Apply the case evidence: Long-term exclusive use, not physical display, places an operating licence among non-current intangible assets, not inventory. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.'] WHERE case_id = 'CASE 6.1.101' AND tier = 'full';
UPDATE public.economics_cases SET tactical_explanations = ARRAY['TRUE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is correct. Although a concession right cannot be touched, it appears among non-current assets rather than current assets because of its long-term value.

The task tests the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Financial statements distinguish stocks (balance sheet at a date) from flows (income and cash over a period); many false statements swap those roles.

Apply the case evidence: Long-term value places an intangible concession right among non-current rather than current assets. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'TRUE — This statement is correct. A concession right is recorded as an intangible non-current asset alongside tangible assets such as buildings and machinery.

This is an accounting reasoning item on the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Decide whether the sentence correctly describes how a transaction, balance-sheet line, or period result affects assets, liabilities, or equity.

From the figures or classification rule involved, intangible and tangible non-current assets, including a concession right, sit within the same balance sheet section. Accounting statements fail when a loss is treated as increasing equity, when a flow is placed on the balance sheet, or when a ratio inequality is reversed.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'TRUE — The claim is correct. The absence of physical form does not prevent a concession right from being classified as a non-current asset.

The task tests the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Financial statements distinguish stocks (balance sheet at a date) from flows (income and cash over a period); many false statements swap those roles.

Apply the case evidence: Physical form is not a requirement for classifying a concession right as non-current. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'TRUE — This statement is correct. A concession right acquired to protect or support a business''s operations over several years is grouped with intangible non-current assets.

This item belongs to the accounting block on the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Ratio and classification questions ask you to connect a figure or comparison in the case with the correct statement category, not merely to recognise a number.

From the figures or classification rule involved, multi-year protective or operational value groups a concession right with intangible non-current assets. Accounting statements fail when a loss is treated as increasing equity, when a flow is placed on the balance sheet, or when a ratio inequality is reversed.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'TRUE — This statement is correct. A concession right is not classified as a current asset because it is not expected to convert into cash within the normal operating cycle.

This is an accounting reasoning item on the balance sheet and classification of assets and liabilities. Decide whether the sentence correctly describes how a transaction, balance-sheet line, or period result affects assets, liabilities, or equity.

From the figures or classification rule involved, because a concession right is not expected to convert into cash within the operating cycle, it is excluded from current assets. Accounting statements fail when a loss is treated as increasing equity, when a flow is placed on the balance sheet, or when a ratio inequality is reversed.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.'] WHERE case_id = 'CASE 6.1.102' AND tier = 'full';
